   id="technology/oozie-recovery-rerun-lab"
   dataFile="/api/content/technology/oozie/data/recovery-rerun-model.json" /%}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Diagnose before retrying or rerunning

A configured automatic retry or a manual-retry resume continues a narrow action
failure boundary. These mechanisms apply to classified action start or end errors;
they do not blindly rerun failed business computation. A workflow rerun creates a new
workflow instance with the same ID and can skip successful nodes. A coordinator rerun
returns selected terminal actions to `WAITING`; `-refresh` re-evaluates dependencies,
while cleanup flags determine what happens to output-event directories.

Before changing job state:

1. Capture the workflow, coordinator, or bundle definition and submitted properties.
2. Save action, server, audit, YARN, and destination logs under the job and action IDs.
3. Classify the outcome as definitely uncommitted, definitely committed, or ambiguous.
4. Patch deterministic defects and validate the XML before a rerun.
5. Select the smallest replay scope that repairs the failed state.
6. Decide whether coordinator output cleanup is safe; `-nocleanup` preserves output
   but can collide with partial or already committed data.
7. Reconcile every external side effect and use stable operation IDs before replay.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/oozie/code/diagnose-and-rerun.sh" language="bash" title="Evidence-first diagnosis and reviewed rerun commands" /%}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Retries and reruns can repeat action code. Neither Oozie HA nor a successful state
transition proves that a remote write happened exactly once.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Operate from job outcomes, not process health alone

Oozie exposes workflow, coordinator, bundle, action, and SLA state through its CLI,
REST API, UI, logs, metrics, notifications, and metadata database. A healthy server can
still have stalled materialization, late data, exhausted launcher capacity, or
repeatedly failing actions.

Monitor at least:

- coordinator actions by `WAITING`, `READY`, `TIMEDOUT`, `SKIPPED`, error, and terminal
  state;
- nominal-to-actual start delay and backlog depth by coordinator;
- workflow and action duration, retry count, error code, and last error node;
- launcher and YARN queue delay, callback failures, and sharelib/classpath errors;
- database latency, command queue depth, JVM saturation, and server availability;
- SLA `START`, `END`, and `DURATION` met or miss events;
- destination freshness, row or object counts, and publication-marker age.

Oozie can emit SLA events through JMS and expose summaries through REST. Its legacy
instrumentation counters can reset with the process, so export durable time series and
alerts outside the Oozie JVM.

For each alert, preserve the nominal slot, coordinator action ID, workflow ID, action
attempts, input URIs, effective configuration, application version, sharelib version,
and destination operation IDs. Those fields turn a generic failure into a replay
decision.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Decide whether to retain or migrate

Oozie retirement changes the platform decision: existing workflows may remain useful,
but the operating team now owns maintenance, dependency compatibility, vulnerability
response, and eventual replacement.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Retain temporarily" eyebrow="Controlled legacy estate" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Keep a bounded estate when its Hadoop integrations are stable, operators understand its
failure modes, dependencies are supportable, and a funded exit plan exists.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Migrate deliberately" eyebrow="Active orchestrator" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Choose Airflow, a managed workflow service, or another orchestrator from interval,
event, isolation, security, backfill, and operations requirements rather than name
recognition.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Avoid literal translation" eyebrow="Preserve semantics" icon="git-compare" tone="violet" %}
Map nominal slots, data predicates, cleanup, retries, kill paths, credentials, and SLA
behavior explicitly. XML nodes and provider operators are not one-to-one contracts.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Airflow, for example, models scheduled runs with data intervals and provides explicit
backfill and reprocessing controls. It is not a drop-in Oozie runtime: coordinator data
availability, bundle operations, sharelib behavior, and rerun cleanup need new designs.

### Roll out a migration without double-writing

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Inventory semantics" label="Discover" %}
Record every nominal interval, dependency marker, parameter source, credential,
side effect, SLA, and operator runbook.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Build contract tests" label="Prove" %}
Replay representative late data, retry, backfill, and ambiguous-commit cases against
versioned fixtures.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Shadow isolated output" label="Compare" %}
Run the replacement against a separate namespace. Compare partitions, counts,
checksums, timing, and failure behavior without publishing twice.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Cut over by interval" label="Control" %}
Assign one scheduler as owner for each nominal slot, ramp concurrency gradually, and
keep a tested rollback path until the observation window closes.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}
{% /section-card %}

